---
import Base from '../../../../layouts/Base.astro';

const SITE = 'https://precisionsolutionstech.com';
const title = 'PrecisDesk Support | Precision Solutions Tech';
const description =
  'Support for PrecisDesk: how to reach us, macOS permissions, where captures are saved, and answers to the questions people ask most.';

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Apps', item: `${SITE}/apps/` },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'PrecisDesk', item: `${SITE}/apps/precisdesk/` },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Support', item: `${SITE}/apps/precisdesk/support/` },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Base
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path="/apps/precisdesk/support/"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  active="apps"
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <ol>
        <li><a href="/">Home</a></li>
        <li><a href="/apps/">Apps</a></li>
        <li><a href="/apps/precisdesk/">PrecisDesk</a></li>
        <li>Support</li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <article class="prose">
      <h1>PrecisDesk Support</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Something not working, or a question the app does not answer? Email
        <a href="mailto:support@precisionsolutionstech.com">support@precisionsolutionstech.com</a>
        and tell us your macOS version, which feature is misbehaving, and what you expected to
        happen. We read every message.
      </p>

      <h2 id="requirements">Requirements</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>macOS 14 (Sonoma) or later</li>
        <li>Apple Silicon or Intel — PrecisDesk is native on both</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="permissions">Permissions</h2>
      <p>
        Most “it isn’t working” reports come down to a macOS permission. PrecisDesk only asks for
        one when you first use the feature that needs it, and features whose permission you decline
        simply do not run.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Screen Recording</strong> — required for screenshots, screen recording, and window
          previews in the Switcher. macOS requires this even for still screenshots.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Accessibility</strong> — required to move and resize other apps’ windows. Capture
          and recording work without it; window management does not.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Microphone</strong> — only to record your voice.</li>
        <li><strong>Camera</strong> — only for webcam picture-in-picture.</li>
        <li><strong>Speech Recognition</strong> — only for automatic captions, which run on-device.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Grant or revoke any of these in <strong>System Settings → Privacy &amp; Security</strong>.
        After changing Screen Recording, quit PrecisDesk completely (⌘Q) and reopen it — macOS only
        applies the change to a fresh launch.
      </p>

      <h2 id="captures">Where your captures go</h2>
      <p>
        PrecisDesk saves to a folder you choose, and lists everything in <strong>History</strong>
        with thumbnails so you never have to go hunting in Finder. You can point screenshots and
        recordings at the same folder or at different ones, set a filename template, and set a
        retention period that clears old captures automatically.
      </p>

      <h2 id="troubleshooting">Common questions</h2>
      <h3>Window snapping does nothing</h3>
      <p>
        Check Accessibility permission first. If it is granted, look at the top of the Windows tab —
        PrecisDesk acts on the window you were last using, and it tells you which one that is. If it
        says no window is chosen, click a window in another app and try again.
      </p>
      <h3>My windows did not come back after unplugging a monitor</h3>
      <p>
        That feature ships <strong>switched off</strong>, because it moves windows you did not ask
        it to move. Turn on <em>Put windows back when my monitors change</em> on the Layouts page.
        It saves your current arrangement the moment you enable it, so there is something to
        restore.
      </p>
      <h3>Captions got a word wrong</h3>
      <p>
        Every caption is editable text — fix the cue under the playhead and update it. You can also
        add cues recognition missed, retime them, and export the lot as an <code>.srt</code> file.
      </p>
      <h3>The webcam is covering something important</h3>
      <p>
        If you recorded with “place in editor after”, move it. Park the playhead where it is in the
        way and drag the camera somewhere else — it stays there from that point onward, so it can
        move during the video. You can also hide it for a stretch entirely.
      </p>

      <h2 id="privacy">Privacy</h2>
      <p>
        PrecisDesk runs on your Mac. Screenshots, recordings, audio, camera video, text recognition
        and speech recognition are all processed locally and never sent to us. There is no account
        system, and no analytics or advertising SDKs. Full detail is in the
        <a href="/legal/privacy/precisdesk/">PrecisDesk Privacy Policy</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
      <p>
        <a href="mailto:support@precisionsolutionstech.com">support@precisionsolutionstech.com</a>
        ·
        <a href="mailto:hello@precisionsolutionstech.com">hello@precisionsolutionstech.com</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/apps/precisdesk/">PrecisDesk overview</a>
        ·
        <a href="/legal/privacy/precisdesk/">Privacy Policy</a>
        ·
        <a href="/about/">About us</a>
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>
